.TH SDR 1
.SH NAME
sdr/fm, sdr/am, sdr/rawiq, sdr/iqhdr \- demodulate a software-defined radio's samples
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sdr/fm
[
.B -nwR
] [
.B -c
.I chans
] [
.B -d
.I µs
] [
.B -g
.I gain
] [
.B -o
.I rate
]
.PP
.B sdr/am
[
.B -R
] [
.B -b
.I hertz
] [
.B -c
.I chans
] [
.B -g
.I gain
] [
.B -o
.I rate
]
.PP
.B sdr/rawiq
.B -r
.I rate
[
.B -f
.I freq
]
.PP
.B sdr/iqhdr
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fm
and
.I am
each read a radio's samples on standard input, as
.IR sdrfs (4)'s
.B iq
gives them: a header, lines of text saying the rate and the encoding
(and the frequency), then 8-bit unsigned I then Q.
The rate in is the header's; input with no header, or in an encoding
other than
.BR cu8 ,
is refused.
They write the sound the samples carry on standard output, as an
.IR audio (6)
file: a header
.RB ( rate ,
.BR chans ,
.BR "bits 16" ,
.BR "enc pcm" ),
in one write, then 16-bit little-endian samples,
.I chans
of each, the same sound in every channel, so
.IR auplay (1)
plays it as it comes.
.B -R
leaves the header out, for
.B /dev/audio
set by hand.
The radio is tuned to the station; what is a little off (a frequency
error, an offset in ppm) is heard all the same.
They are filters: they read until end of file and keep no state
elsewhere.
A retune ends
.BR iq 's
stream, and so them.
.PP
.I Fm
demodulates FM: wide, broadcast FM, by default (or
.BR -w ),
and narrow with
.B -n
(marine VHF, 2 m amateur, NOAA weather, the land mobile bands).
The samples are summed to about 240k a second; narrow, the channel
(8 kHz either side) is filtered out of them and they are decimated to
about 48k.
The angle each turns from the last is the sound.
Wide, it is de-emphasised,
.B -d
microseconds: 50, as broadcast in most of the world, 75 in the Americas,
0 none (narrow has none unless asked).
Wide, the sound is kept below 15 kHz (above are the stereo pilot and
subcarriers, and RDS), and below 0.4 of the rate out; it is mono.
Full deviation (75 kHz wide, 5 kHz narrow) is 0.7 of full scale.
.PP
.I Am
demodulates AM: the air band (118 to 137 MHz), and the short and medium
wave broadcasts through direct sampling or an upconverter.
The samples are summed to about 240k a second, the channel
.RB ( -b
hertz either side, 5000) is filtered out and they are decimated to
about 48k.
Each one's magnitude over the carrier's (its magnitude averaged over a
tenth of a second) is the sound, so the level out does not follow the
signal's strength; full modulation is 0.7 of full scale.
.PP
Both take:
.TP
.BI -o " rate"
the rate out: 8000 by default; higher for broadcast FM
.RI ( audio (3)
takes 8000, 11025, 22050 and 44100).
.TP
.BI -c " chans"
the channels out (2, as
.IR audio (3)).
.TP
.BI -g " gain"
the volume multiplied; past full scale is clipped.
.PP
A rate out the rate in cannot give is refused.
.PP
.I Rawiq
puts a header on samples that have none (a recording by the host's
.IR rtl_sdr ,
or samples with their header cut off): it writes one saying
.B -r
samples a second,
.BR "enc cu8" ,
and
.B -f
hertz if given (k, M and G taken), then copies its standard input.
.PP
.I Iqhdr
reads only the header of samples on standard input (or in
.IR file )
and prints its lines, one a line, without the padding or the empty line
after them; each is a command
.BR sdrfs 's
.B ctl
takes, so written there they tune the radio as the samples were tuned.
Input with no header is refused.
.SH EXAMPLES
A broadcast station, to the speaker:
.IP
.EX
; sdrfs source localhost
; echo 'freq 98.5M
rate 2.4M
gain 20' > /mnt/sdr/ctl
; sdr/fm -o 44100 < /mnt/sdr/iq | auplay /fd/0
.EE
.PP
The air band's emergency frequency:
.IP
.EX
; echo freq 121.5M > /mnt/sdr/ctl
; sdr/am < /mnt/sdr/iq | auplay /fd/0
.EE
.PP
A recording made once, demodulated later, and the radio tuned back to
it:
.IP
.EX
; cat /mnt/sdr/iq > /tmp/marine.iq
; sdr/fm -n -c 1 < /tmp/marine.iq > /tmp/marine.au
; sdr/iqhdr < /tmp/marine.iq > /mnt/sdr/ctl
.EE
.PP
One made by the host's
.IR rtl_sdr ,
which has no header:
.IP
.EX
; sdr/rawiq -r 2.4M -f 98.5M < /tmp/rtl.cu8 | sdr/fm -o 44100 > /tmp/fm.au
.EE
